<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

`AshPhoenix.Form` builds a form **from an action**. The fields it accepts, the validations it runs, the errors it shows and the policies it obeys all come from that action. You do not write a schema, a changeset function or an error translator.

Here is the lesson 10 domain again, self-contained, with one addition: a `string_length` validation on `:open` — the first rule that lives in the action rather than the attribute:

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Support,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    authorizers: [Ash.Policy.Authorizer]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :subject, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :status, :atom, constraints: [one_of: [:open, :closed]], default: :open, public?: true
    attribute :owner_id, :uuid, public?: true
    create_timestamp :inserted_at, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :open do
      accept [:subject, :owner_id]
      validate string_length(:subject, min: 5)
    end

    update :close, change: set_attribute(:status, :closed)

    read :open_tickets do
      filter expr(status == :open)
      pagination keyset?: true, default_limit: 20, required?: false
    end
  end

  policies do
    policy action_type(:read) do
      authorize_if expr(owner_id == ^actor(:id))
    end

    policy action_type([:create, :update]) do
      authorize_if actor_present()
    end
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.Support do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
      define :open_ticket, action: :open, args: [:subject]
      define :list_open, action: :open_tickets
      define :close_ticket, action: :close
    end
  end
end
```

(If you still have lesson 10's notebook open, editing *its* cell works too — but re-run that cell; a second cell defining the same module is a compile error, see lesson 10.)

## Build and validate a form

```elixir
actor = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}

form =
  Tutorial.Support.Ticket
  |> AshPhoenix.Form.for_create(:open, actor: actor, params: %{"owner_id" => actor.id})

form = AshPhoenix.Form.validate(form, %{"subject" => "Hi"})
{form.valid?, AshPhoenix.Form.errors(form)}
```

The error came from `validate string_length/2` in the action, not from anything in the UI.

Submit a valid version of the form and get the created ticket back.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  {:ok, ticket} =
    form
    |> AshPhoenix.Form.validate(%{"subject" => "Printer on fire", "owner_id" => actor.id})
    |> AshPhoenix.Form.submit()

  ticket.subject
  ```

  </div>
</details>

## In a LiveView

```elixir
defmodule NewTicketLive do
  use Phoenix.LiveView

  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
    actor = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}
    form = AshPhoenix.Form.for_create(Tutorial.Support.Ticket, :open, actor: actor) |> to_form()
    {:ok, assign(socket, current_user: actor, form: form, created: nil)}
  end

  def handle_event("validate", %{"form" => params}, socket) do
    {:noreply, assign(socket, form: AshPhoenix.Form.validate(socket.assigns.form, params))}
  end

  def handle_event("save", %{"form" => params}, socket) do
    params = Map.put(params, "owner_id", socket.assigns.current_user.id)

    case AshPhoenix.Form.submit(socket.assigns.form, params: params) do
      {:ok, ticket} -> {:noreply, assign(socket, created: ticket.subject)}
      {:error, form} -> {:noreply, assign(socket, form: form)}
    end
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~H"""
    <.form for={@form} phx-change="validate" phx-submit="save">
      <input name={@form[:subject].name} value={@form[:subject].value} />
      <p :for={{msg, _} <- @form[:subject].errors}>{msg}</p>
      <button>Open ticket</button>
    </.form>
    <p :if={@created}>Created: {@created}</p>
    """
  end
end

PhoenixPlayground.start(live: NewTicketLive, port: 4011, open_browser: false)
```

Building the form in `mount/3` is fine: it is a struct, not a query.

## Exercise: a close button that respects the action

Add a `close` button. Rather than `Ash.Changeset.for_update(:close)` in the event handler, use `AshPhoenix.Form.for_update(ticket, :close, actor: ...)` and submit it with `params: %{}` — the option is now effectively required, and omitting it prints a deprecation warning that looks like your mistake. Then add `validate attribute_equals(:status, :open)` to `:close` and watch a double close fail with an error you can render.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  update :close do
    validate attribute_equals(:status, :open), message: "is already closed"
    change set_attribute(:status, :closed)
  end
  ```

  ```elixir
  {:ok, closed} =
    AshPhoenix.Form.for_update(ticket, :close, actor: actor) |> AshPhoenix.Form.submit(params: %{})

  {:error, form} =
    AshPhoenix.Form.for_update(closed, :close, actor: actor) |> AshPhoenix.Form.submit(params: %{})

  AshPhoenix.Form.errors(form)
  ```

  The rule "you cannot close a closed ticket" now holds for the UI, the API, an agent and a background job alike. Keep that in mind in lesson 21: a BPMN gateway is **not** where this rule goes.

  </div>
</details>

